.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause
.Dd September 6, 2026
.Dt SYS-INIT-BUILD-NATIVE-SERVICE-WORKER 1
.Os sys-init experimental
.Sh NAME
.Nm sys-init-build-native-service-worker
.Nd build an inert experimental Aether artifact from local inputs
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm python3
.Ar scripts/build-native-service-worker.py
.br
.Fl Fl sdk Ar directory
.br
.Fl Fl tools-lock Ar file
.br
.Fl Fl receipt-pin Ar digest
.br
.Fl Fl output Ar directory
.Nm python3
.Ar scripts/build-native-service-worker.py
.br
.Fl Fl help
.Nm python3
.Ar scripts/build-native-service-worker.py
.br
.Fl Fl version
.Sh DESCRIPTION
This source-checkout developer tool builds files only; it does not boot a
guest,
install a service or execute the generated image.
Use Python 3.10 or newer on a
POSIX development host, from the repository root.
No root privileges are needed.
A successful build establishes the recorded structural checks, not native
runtime
acceptance.
Use the separately qualified runner and matching producer profile
before making a target-behavior claim.
.Pp
Supply an independently reviewed local I/O SDK receipt and its exact SHA-256,
plus the tools-lock file selected by that receipt.
The SDK must contain
evidence.json, stage/usr/include and stage/usr/lib with crt1.o, crti.o, crtn.o
and libc.a.
The receipt must select probe_variant io.
The tools lock identifies
clang, lld, nm, readelf and objdump by executable path and SHA-256.
The builder verifies receipt, lock and libc identities before creating output,
copies local inputs and checks used tool hashes before and after their runs.
These executable hashes do not authenticate SDK provenance or shared
libraries.
.Pp
Compilation targets x86_64-linux-musl using C11, optimization, stack
protection,
explicit copied headers and an empty sysroot; linking is static without
default
libraries.
ELF checks require the expected x86-64 target, TLS, non-executable
stack, no dynamic interpreter/segment, no writable-executable load segment,
no undefined external symbols and no remaining relocations.
This is a scoped
structural check of trusted build outputs, not a general hostile-ELF
validator.
The service worker links directly against the captured libc/startup objects;
it does not consume native_io.o or produce a native-C envelope.
.Pp
Each compiler/tool invocation has a 180-second deadline and a 4 MiB
merged-output
limit.
Failure kills only the owned group of trusted, non-detaching tools and
retains the accepted log prefix.
The command is not confinement against hostile
tools or concurrent input mutation.
Copying, hashing and other Python work are
outside this per-tool deadline.
.Sh OPTIONS AND OPERANDS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl Fl sdk Ar directory
Required local SDK root with the reviewed files described above.
.It Fl Fl tools-lock Ar file
Required tool-lock JSON whose digest matches the SDK receipt.
.It Fl Fl receipt-pin Ar digest
Required exact SHA-256 of SDK evidence.json.
Obtain it from the reviewed input
handoff; computing an arbitrary local digest does not establish trust.
.It Fl Fl output Ar directory
Required new output directory.
It must not exist.
Parents may be created;
the builder sets the new root mode to 0700.
Partial failure artifacts remain.
.It Fl Fl help
Print usage and exit without building or creating output.
.It Fl Fl version
Print the builder-command version and exit; not a published release identity.
.El
.Sh OUTPUT AND FILES
The successful command prints an artifact path on standard output.
Compiler/linker output is retained in logs.
Python and argument errors use
standard error and may include tracebacks.
Preserve the entire output directory,
including failed attempts; a running/missing receipt is not a successful
build.
Output includes inputs/, service-worker.o, service-worker.elf,
service-worker.map, evidence.json, attempts.json and named tool logs.
The final record uses schema sys-init-native-service-worker-v1 and result
pass.
Attempt failures use result fail with error_type and error, including timeout,
overflow and interruption; they do not use the I/O builder's separate result
names.
.Pp
A post-compile size or structural failure can follow a successful tool
attempt.
Check the builder exit and final evidence, not just the compiler receipt.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
Tools receive an explicit environment: PATH=/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin,
LC_ALL=C and TMPDIR inside the new output directory.
Compiler/tool executable
paths come from the copied lock.
Other caller tool variables are not forwarded.
The parent Python process still uses its normal host environment.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It 0
The build and its final checks completed; also supported help/version queries.
.It 1
An explicit build rejection or ordinary uncaught tool/Python failure.
Signal or abrupt termination can produce other statuses without final
evidence.
.It 64
Invalid arguments.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
From the repository root, inspect usage without launching tools:
.Bd -literal -offset left
python3 scripts/build-native-service-worker.py --help
.Ed
.Pp
Set SDK, TOOLS_LOCK and RECEIPT_PIN from an independently reviewed local SDK
handoff first.
They are shell placeholders, not builder environment settings.
Choose an output path that does not yet exist:
.Bd -literal -offset left
python3 scripts/build-native-service-worker.py \e
  --sdk "$SDK" --tools-lock "$TOOLS_LOCK" \e
  --receipt-pin "$RECEIPT_PIN" \e
  --output out/service-worker-review
.Ed
.Pp
Expected success is an artifact path and exit zero.
These examples do not boot
or execute the artifact.
Do not reuse paths containing retained acceptance data.
.Sh SEE ALSO
Repository guides: docs/NATIVE-SERVICE.md, docs/QUALITY-PROCESS.md,
docs/DOCUMENTATION-INVENTORY.md and the source script for exact build flags.
.Sh CAVEATS
The manual title is not an installed command.
Independent technical/reader
review and target manual lookup remain pending.
Build success is not runtime
conformance or authority to publish a release.
